            "en": {
                    "description": "Asterix im Morgenland is a comic-based action adventure that sends Asterix and Obelix to India to restore Cacofonix's voice and rescue Princess Orinjade.",
                    "history": "Based on the Asterix comic of the same name, the game sends Asterix, Obelix and Cacofonix on an unusual journey to India. There, the bard's singing is intended to summon rain and end a severe drought. When he loses his voice on the way, Asterix and Obelix must first find a cure while also trying to save Princess Orinjade. The game combines adventure and action sequences shown from various perspectives, requiring both correct decisions and dexterity. The German C64 version appeared in 1987 and is among Coktel Vision's early Asterix adaptations.",
                    "review": "Asterix im Morgenland looks extremely appealing at first. The characters are instantly recognisable, many scenes clearly reflect the comics, and Asterix fans will recognise familiar ideas. The game itself is less convincing. Many situations consist of simple choices or short action sequences whose logic is not always clear, creating the impression of completing disconnected scenes rather than playing a coherent adventure. Some decisions rely heavily on trial and error. As an Asterix curiosity, however, it retains a certain charm and its colourful imagery captures some of the comics' atmosphere.",
                    "trivia": [
                        "The game is based on the 1987 comic Asterix and the Magic Carpet, originally titled Astérix chez Rahàzade.",
                        "It appeared internationally under several titles, including Asterix and the Magic Carpet and Astérix chez Rahàzade.",
                        "The C64 credits attribute the cover illustration to Albert Uderzo, long-time artist and co-creator of Asterix."
                    ],
                    "worthPlaying": "Asterix im Morgenland is still primarily worthwhile for Asterix enthusiasts and collectors of unusual comic adaptations. The game has clear weaknesses, but its closeness to the source and authentic appearance make it an interesting piece of late-1980s licensed-game history."
                }
