            "en": {
                    "description": "Battlehawks 1942 is a historical combat-flight simulation portraying the Pacific air war from both American and Japanese perspectives.",
                    "history": "Battlehawks 1942 places you in the Second World War's Pacific theatre on both the American and Japanese sides. Missions reflect historical battles including the Coral Sea, Midway, the Eastern Solomons and the Santa Cruz Islands. Depending on the assignment, you pilot fighters, dive bombers or torpedo bombers to intercept aircraft, attack ships or escort friendly formations. The game combines flight simulation with accessible action while placing great emphasis on historical atmosphere. A particularly notable feature allowed completed missions to be replayed from several camera perspectives.",
                    "review": "Battlehawks 1942 is less a dry flight simulation than an accessible blend of simulation and action, which allows it to work surprisingly well today. Controls remain manageable while missions offer enough variety to avoid becoming simple take-off and landing exercises. Its Pacific-war atmosphere is especially strong, with aircraft, ships and scenarios visibly based on historical counterparts. The Amiga version has naturally aged, particularly its simple landscapes. Nevertheless, Battlehawks retains considerable charm and already displays ideas Lawrence Holland later developed in Their Finest Hour and Secret Weapons of the Luftwaffe.",
                    "trivia": [
                        "Battlehawks 1942 was designed and programmed by Lawrence Holland, with additional design by Noah Falstein.",
                        "It began a trilogy of Lucasfilm combat-flight simulations continued by Their Finest Hour and Secret Weapons of the Luftwaffe.",
                        "The extensive manual contained substantial historical material about the Pacific War, going far beyond a conventional game guide."
                    ],
                    "worthPlaying": "Battlehawks 1942 remains especially worthwhile for admirers of classic flight simulations and historical war games. Its combination of approachable controls, historical missions and varied aircraft makes it more accessible than many simulations of its era. It is also an important early milestone in the development of Lawrence Holland's later celebrated flight simulations."
                }
