            "en": {
                    "description": "Börsenfieber is a realistically designed business simulation in which up to four players trade shares, funds, gold and other investments in an attempt to turn their starting capital into one million German marks.",
                    "history": "Börsenfieber was published for the Amiga by Falken-Verlag in 1989 as a mixture of stock-market simulation and educational programme, and is internationally known as Wall$treet. It initially tests the player's financial knowledge, with stronger results granting starting capital from 5,000 to 50,000 DM. The goal is to amass one million marks within no more than 99 weeks or rounds. Available investments include shares, funds, foreign currencies, gold and savings products. More than 140 economic and political news events affect prices and demand accurate interpretation of market movements. A separate portfolio manager could even track real securities holdings.",
                    "review": "Börsenfieber is the kind of business simulation in which tables, news and figures matter more than animation. Anyone expecting spectacular graphics has certainly come to the wrong exchange. Behind the dry presentation is a serious attempt to teach securities trading through play. Political and economic reports genuinely affect prices, creating meaningful pressure when making decisions, while a ten-second limit adds urgency to share transactions. The controls and presentation now feel antiquated, but the game possesses substance as both an educational programme and a simulation. It is a compelling period document for admirers of old German business games.",
                    "trivia": [
                        "Börsenfieber is also known internationally as Wall$treet and appeared on C64, Atari ST and DOS as well as Amiga.",
                        "More than 140 economic and political reports can influence its simulated financial markets.",
                        "A separate portfolio manager allowed players to track real securities holdings and calculate profits and losses."
                    ],
                    "worthPlaying": "Börsenfieber remains worthwhile chiefly for admirers of classic business simulations and as an unusual piece of German software history. It genuinely attempts to explain the fundamentals of securities trading rather than using finance merely as scenery. Players more interested in tables, economics and long-term decisions than action may still enjoy it."
                }
