            "en": {
                    "description": "Fighting Steel is a detailed real-time Second World War naval-combat simulation in which players command individual warships or complete task forces.",
                    "history": "Fighting Steel was developed for Strategic Simulations by Divide By Zero Software and released for Windows in 1999. It focuses on surface engagements in the Atlantic and Pacific from 1939 to 1942, offering American, British, German and Japanese vessels. Players command ships or formations and control course, speed, targeting and artillery. More than ninety ship classes are represented. Ten historical scenarios are joined by four connected campaigns, a battle generator and scenario editor. Unlike SSI's older Great Naval Battles series, engagements are rendered entirely in three dimensions, combining traditional tactical naval warfare with a direct visual depiction.",
                    "review": "Fighting Steel clearly targets players who want to know not merely that a cruiser has guns, but their calibre, range and fire-control solution. It offers impressive depth and represents large naval engagements more credibly than action-oriented games. Commanding formations and balancing position, speed and firepower are especially compelling. The problems lie in execution: the interface is complex, many procedures are awkward and the original release suffered serious stability issues. Behind those difficulties is a demanding and interesting naval simulation. Players willing to study it and accept its rough edges receive much more than attractive ships on blue water.",
                    "trivia": [
                        "Fighting Steel contains more than ninety ship classes from the American, British, German and Japanese navies.",
                        "Alongside ten historical scenarios, it includes four campaigns, a battle generator and scenario editor.",
                        "After official development ended, Naval Warfare Simulations received permission from SSI and Divide By Zero to create free expansions and improvements."
                    ],
                    "worthPlaying": "Fighting Steel remains most worthwhile for admirers of historical naval simulations. Detailed vessels, tactical fleet command and custom engagements provide depth rarely found in modern naval games. The interface and original technical problems require patience, but committed players receive an unusually serious simulation of large surface-gunnery battles."
                }
