            "en": {
                    "description": "All New World of Lemmings is the third major game in the classic series and sends three surviving tribes through ninety new puzzle levels.",
                    "history": "All New World of Lemmings was developed by DMA Design, published by Psygnosis and released for DOS in 1994. In North America it was titled The Lemmings Chronicles. Continuing Lemmings 2: The Tribes, it reduces the previous twelve tribes to Classic, Egyptian and Shadow, each with its own visual theme and thirty levels. The familiar objective remains guiding a group safely from entrance to exit, but the skill system is substantially changed. Some abilities are collected as objects within levels and can be carried or dropped by individual Lemmings. Every Lemming also has basic actions including jumping, turning and blocking. A replay feature repeats previous commands and permits changes from a chosen point. David Jones produced the game, with DOS programming by Russel Elliot, Robert Parsons, Keith R. Hamilton and Steven Reid.",
                    "review": "All New World of Lemmings attempts something risky for an established series: changing the rules without discarding its identity. The revised skill system places greater emphasis on individual Lemmings and less on simply issuing commands from a toolbar. This makes many levels more interesting, although it can also demand more attention to the controls than desired. The three tribes are visually distinct and ninety levels provide ample rescue work. Some of the original's immediate clarity is lost, but players who still wanted more green-haired collective peril after Lemmings 2 receive a clever and independent third instalment.",
                    "trivia": [
                        "In North America the game was released as The Lemmings Chronicles.",
                        "It contains ninety levels divided equally between the Classic, Egyptian and Shadow tribes.",
                        "Dave Pether created the cover illustration, while David Osborne served as art director."
                    ],
                    "worthPlaying": "All New World of Lemmings remains most worthwhile for players already familiar with the classic series who want a different interpretation. Its revised skills and three distinct settings provide sufficient identity while retaining the essential Lemmings feel. As the conclusion of DMA Design's classic era, it is also historically interesting."
                }
